---
import Section from '../ui/Section.astro';
import Stack from '../layout/Stack.astro';
import Grid from '../layout/Grid.astro';
import Point from '../ui/Point.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../ui/CodeBlock.astro';
import Table from '../ui/Table.astro';

const workflow = `<span class="prompt">$</span> liam build
  <span class="success">built</span> acme.oci     6.1 MB + site     <span class="comment">container image</span>
  <span class="success">built</span> acme.fc     15.2 MB + site     <span class="comment">vmlinux + initramfs for Firecracker</span>
  <span class="success">built</span> acme.efi    15.4 MB + site     <span class="comment">unified kernel image for cloud VMs and metal</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> liam run                          <span class="comment"># boots locally: microVM on Linux, container on macOS</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> liam deploy --target aws          <span class="comment"># A/B slot swap, rolls back if /healthz fails</span>`;
---

<Section id="deploy" eyebrow="Deploy" title="Build once. Run it anywhere with a kernel.">
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    One build produces every target you list, from the same binary and the same site pack. What you test locally is
    byte for byte what runs in production.
  </Fragment>
  <Stack gap="7">
    <CodeBlock title="terminal" note="the whole workflow" output={workflow} />
    <Table
      caption="Deploy targets"
      columns={['Target', 'Artifact', 'Runs on', 'How it boots']}
      rows={[
        ['Container', '<code>.oci</code>', 'Docker, Kubernetes, Fly.io, Cloud Run, ECS', "liam-init as PID 1 on the host's kernel"],
        ['microVM', '<code>.fc</code>', 'Firecracker, Cloud Hypervisor', 'PVH direct boot, initramfs built into the kernel'],
        ['Cloud VM', '<code>.efi</code> disk', 'AWS, GCP, Hetzner, any UEFI host', 'Unified kernel image with A/B slots'],
        ['Bare metal', '<code>.efi</code>', 'x86_64 and aarch64 servers', 'Same image, from disk or iPXE'],
      ]}
    />
    <Grid cols={3} gap="6">
      <Point title="Metrics and logs">
        Prometheus metrics on a separate admin port. JSON logs to stdout or the serial console. OpenTelemetry traces when
        you turn them on.
      </Point>
      <Point title="Upgrades without downtime">
        liam-init hands the listening sockets to the new liamd, and the old one drains. On VMs and metal the new image
        goes into the idle slot and boots only if it passes health checks.
      </Point>
      <Point title="Bare metal, honestly">
        Server firmware takes seconds before any OS runs. liam cannot change that, but everything after firmware handoff
        is held to the same budgets.
      </Point>
    </Grid>
  </Stack>
</Section>
